      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Вы хотите войну?",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'Do you want a war?' Notice how 'you' sets up the clause and leads naturally into 'the war'.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Будет вам война.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'There will be a war for you.' Dmitry combines 'will be' with 'a war' in a natural Russian turn of phrase.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Алло, добрый день.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'Hello, good afternoon.' Notice how 'hello?' sets up the clause and leads naturally into 'day'.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "За что вам платят деньги?",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'For what do they pay you money?' Dmitry uses the polite plural 'you' alongside 'money', keeping a formal or respectful register.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Вы куда смотрите?",
